---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(220);
  ellipse(200, 200, 60, 60);
}`;
const Figure2 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
}

function draw() {
  background(220);
  ellipse(200, 200, 60, 60);
}`;
const Figure3 = `let x = 0;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
}

function draw() {
  background(220);
  ellipse(x, 200, 60, 60);
  x = x + 2;
}`;
const Figure4 = `let x = 0;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
}

function draw() {
  // no background() here
  ellipse(x, 200, 60, 60);
  x = x + 2;
}`;
const Figure5 = `let x = 0;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
}

function draw() {
  background(220);
  ellipse(x, 200, 60, 60);
  x = x + 2;

  if (x > 400) {
    x = 0;
  }
}`;
const Task1 = `let x = 0;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
}

function draw() {
  background(30);

  fill(120, 200, 255);
  ellipse(x, 200, 50, 50);

  x = x + 2;
}
`;
const Task2 = `let x = 0;
let y = 200;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
}

function draw() {
  background(30);

  fill(120, 200, 255);
  ellipse(x, y, 50, 50);

  x = x + 2;

  if (x > 400) {
    x = 0;
  }
}
`;
const Task3 = `let x = 0;
let y = 200;
let speed = 2;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
}

function draw() {
  background(30);

  fill(120, 200, 255);
  ellipse(x, y, 50, 50);

  x = x + speed;

  if (x > 400) {
    x = 0;
  }

  // Make the circle change color once it passes the halfway mark
  // Add a second circle that moves the opposite way, right to left
  //   give it its own position variable
  //   wrap it back to the right edge when it goes off the left
}
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.17: p5 Lab 4 &mdash; The Loop You Didn't Write</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.17</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">p5 Lab 4 &mdash; The Loop You Didn't Write</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>draw() Runs Over and Over</p>
              <p>Animation Is Iteration</p>
              <p>Clearing the Canvas</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Something Was Missing</h2>
              <p>
                Labs 1 through 3 put everything inside <CodeSnippet code="setup()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, which
                runs <b>once</b>. Nothing you have drawn has ever moved.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Something Was Missing</h2>
              <p>
                That was on purpose. You needed loops first &mdash; because the
                thing that makes a sketch move <i>is</i> a loop.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Functions</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo — setup() only"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">Runs once. One circle. Done.</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Two Functions</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo — with draw()"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                <CodeSnippet code="draw()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> runs <b class="text-sn-theme">about 60 times
                a second, forever</b>, from the moment setup finishes.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Did Not Write This Loop</h2>
              <p>
                There is no <CodeSnippet code="while" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> anywhere. p5 calls{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="draw()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> for you, over and over.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                It is still iteration. The three parts from Activity 1.16 are all
                there &mdash; setup before, a body that repeats, and a change each
                pass. p5 just supplies the loop itself.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Making It Move</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                One variable, changed a little on every pass. That is the whole
                trick behind every animation you have ever seen.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Where the Variable Lives</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet code="let x = 0;" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> is <b class="text-sn-theme">outside
                both functions</b>, at the top of the file.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Put it inside <CodeSnippet code="draw()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and it gets reset to 0 on
                every pass &mdash; sixty times a second, forever, never moving.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Why background() Is Inside draw()</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo — background removed"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Without it, nothing erases the previous frame. You get a smear
                across the canvas instead of a moving circle.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Why background() Is Inside draw()</h2>
              <p>
                Each pass paints on top of the last one. <CodeSnippet code="background()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />{" "}
                wipes the canvas clean first.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Try removing it at least once. The smear is a genuinely useful
                thing to have seen, and some people like it enough to keep it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Wrapping Around</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure5}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                A conditional inside a loop, checking a boundary. Everything from
                Activities 1.11 and 1.13, now running sixty times a second.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Explaining what draw() does and how often</li>
                <li>Declaring a variable that survives between passes</li>
                <li>Animating by changing a variable each pass</li>
                <li>Using a conditional to handle a boundary</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Frame</dt>
                  <dd>One pass of draw(), producing one picture.</dd>
                  <dt>Frame rate</dt>
                  <dd>How many frames are drawn per second, around 60 by default.</dd>
                  <dt>Global variable</dt>
                  <dd>
                    A variable declared outside every function, so it keeps its
                    value between passes.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Animation loop</dt>
                  <dd>
                    A loop that redraws a scene repeatedly to create movement.
                  </dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>p5.js</b> sketch named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">1-17-motion</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: One Moving Circle</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it. The circle should slide right and vanish off the edge.</li>
            <li>
              Now move <CodeSnippet code="let x = 0;" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> inside{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="draw()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and run again. It should stop moving
              entirely. Work out why, then put it back.
            </li>
            <li>
              Then comment out <CodeSnippet code="background(30);" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and run once more.
              Put it back afterward.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 4 — The Loop You Didn't Write"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Wrap It Around</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a <CodeSnippet code="y" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> variable and a boundary check so the circle
              reappears on the left instead of disappearing.
            </li>
            <li>
              Try <CodeSnippet code="x > 400" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> versus{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="x > 425" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and watch the difference at the edge. The
              circle has width, which the boundary has to allow for.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 4 — The Loop You Didn't Write"
            ins={{ range: "2, 12, 16-18" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Add Color and a Second Circle</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              A <CodeSnippet code="speed" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> variable has been added so you can tune the
              motion. Try 1, then 8.
            </li>
            <li>
              The comments describe two additions. Write the code under them.
            </li>
            <li>
              The second circle moves <i>left</i>, so its change is a subtraction
              and its wrap check looks at the other edge.
            </li>
            <li>
              Give it its own position variable at the top of the file, next to
              the others. It must not share <CodeSnippet code="x" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 4 — The Loop You Didn't Write"
            ins={{ range: "3, 15, 21-24" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): Bounce Instead of Wrap</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Make a circle reverse direction at each edge rather than wrapping
              around.
            </li>
            <li>
              Hint: the speed itself can be a variable, and a variable can be
              made negative.
            </li>
            <li>
              This is the classic first p5 animation, and it is worth the
              struggle. Two conditionals and one sign change.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your finished sketch should show all of the following.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>A circle moving smoothly left to right, wrapping around forever.</li>
            <li>That circle changing color once it passes the middle.</li>
            <li>
              A second circle moving right to left, wrapping the other way, at
              its own position.
            </li>
            <li>No smearing &mdash; the canvas clears every frame.</li>
            <li>
              Changing <CodeSnippet code="speed" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> changes how fast the first circle
              travels.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            You should be able to say where the loop is, even though you never
            wrote one.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Moving <code>let x = 0;</code> inside <code>draw()</code> stopped
              the animation. Explain exactly what happened on each pass.
            </li>
            <li>
              Activity 1.16 said every loop has a setup, a test, and a change.
              Point to each of those in this sketch, including the ones p5
              provides.
            </li>
            <li>
              Removing <code>background()</code> leaves a trail. Describe what
              that tells you about how the canvas actually works.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
